  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its first check came back `ok`, meaning it ran and the 5xx count was not over 10.\n\n**1. The data.** The `kibana_sample_data_logs` index holds about 14,000 web requests. Each one records:\n- the time (`timestamp`), client IP, host and URL\n- the response code: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441)\n- bytes transferred, file extension, operating system and user agent\n- the source and destination country, and coordinates\n\nThe sample timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, so part of the data is dated in the future.\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-a0b3e964 Web logs overview\"** ([open it](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-a0b3e964-web-logs-overview-e89449b534d4)). It has 7 panels and defaults to the last 7 days:\n- Total requests (single number)\n- Unique visitors (count of distinct client IPs)\n- Requests over time, split by response code (bar chart)\n- Bytes transferred over time (line chart)\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 source countries (bar chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and bytes (table)\n\nI confirmed the dashboard and its 7 panels were saved, but I haven't opened it to check that the charts actually show data.\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-a0b3e964 5xx spike\"** (id `4aad0694-27cc-4666-b39e-6fa1dccf0125`)\n- It is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" and fires when more than 10 arrive within 5 minutes.\n- The rule has no actions attached, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana; nobody gets notified. Tell me where you'd like alerts sent (for example Slack or email) and I can set that up.",
